<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis-Alexandre Berthier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1753–1815</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis-Alexandre Berthier was a French marshal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s chief of staff from 1796 to 1814. He occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1798, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> was proclaimed, and organised the staff work behind Napoleon's campaigns. Napoleon made him Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a> in 1806 and Prince of Wagram in 1809. He rallied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a> in 1814 and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-alexandre-berthier.jpg" alt="Louis-Alexandre Berthier" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques Augustin Catherine Pajou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis-Alexandre_Berthier.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
